{6} \\\\  & =7\\,140.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389818 #edb/ref 1071 "How many different ways can a group of four be made from a room of eighty people?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bfaea9e-ab13-55ff-a72c-f7e351269ac2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389818 #edb/ref 1074 "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $80$ people, so $n=80.$ We want to select $4$ of those people for each group, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential groups of four is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{80}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{80!}{(80-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80!}{76!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77\\cdot \\cancel{76!}}{\\cancel{76!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{37\\,957\\,920}{24} \\\\  & =1\\,581\\,580.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389819 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0c7c9200-9af1-5711-bd23-c6a6abc5a745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389820 #edb/ref 1080 "7\\,957\\,9